513
00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:47.423
It's such an interesting, weird,

514
00:20:49.516 --> 00:20:50.349
like clash of cultures.

515
00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:53.310
Like, I only know all of this through John's lens.

516
00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.220
I've never met Harley, but John talks about.

517
00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:56.510
I mean, John, you know, grew up

518
00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:59.410
in and out of all kinds of foster homes

519
00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:01.880
and his life story's insane.

520
00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:06.390
But he falls into this scene and kind of is taken

521
00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.870
under the Bad Brains' wing.

522
00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:10.637
And that's where he learned kind of about Ital

523
00:21:12.485 --> 00:21:13.490
and like all of these ideas

524
00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:15.670
about treating the body temple.

525
00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:18.710
I think the Krishna aspect came a little bit later.

526
00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:22.120
But the food aspect of his culture

527
00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:24.640
becomes front and center super important.

528
00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:28.400
He's working in some vegetarian restaurant

529
00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:29.950
or a food market or something like that.

530
00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:32.510
And I know like Angelica's Kitchen

531
00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.390
was like a big kind of focal point.

532
00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:35.570
And I was at a place called the Himsa.

533
00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:39.710
That's where I worked and Porcell, our guitar player,

534
00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:44.217
worked at prAna, and there was a bookstore called Ayurveda.

535
00:21:44.217 --> 00:21:48.090
Now we just went to Joy Cafe, incredible cafe.

536
00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:53.090
And even yoga, it's just like wasn't in the conversation.

537
00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:54.890
Veganism, animal rights, vegetarian.

538
00:21:54.890 --> 00:21:56.760
It wasn't in the conversation.

539
00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:58.500
But you know the Lower East Side

540
00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:03.500
was a weird subculture of freaks basically.

541
00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:06.040
And whether you were into punk, or hardcore,

542
00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:08.600
death, or goth, or vegetarianism,

543
00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:11.110
there was all weird, just peculiar people

544
00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:13.400
would gravitate towards the Lower East Side.

545
00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:15.150
And we all sort of accepted each other

546
00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.873
as we're all bunch of outcasts here, you know.

547
00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:22.840
So yeah, there was these random Angelica's Kitchens

548
00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:25.457
and we all sort of grew up interested in...

549
00:22:25.457 --> 00:22:29.817
I mean I remember just eating spirulina in 1985

550
00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:34.188
when no one know what spirulina was.

551
00:22:34.188 --> 00:22:35.021
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

552
00:22:35.021 --> 00:22:37.760
But where does the Hare Krishna thing intersect with that?

553
00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.540
Was that community just living in the same neighborhood?

554
00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:45.100
How does the like straight edge hardcore scene

555
00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:46.673
in the Venn diagram, you know what I mean?

556
00:22:46.673 --> 00:22:48.150
Like it's one thing to be punk,

557
00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:49.770
it's another thing to be straight edge,

558
00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:52.530
and then to just gravitate towards

559
00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:55.950
the Hare Krishna movement and follow the Bhakti path.

560
00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.500
It seems like the straight edge community

561
00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.760
is a little bit of a feeder to that.

562
00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:03.800
Like if you telescope out, it's like

563
00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:05.050
all right, well these are about,

564
00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:06.730
this is about like taking a stand

565
00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:09.320
against mainstream society.

566
00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:11.930
We're not buying into this paradigm.

567
00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:14.020
we're looking for something more meaningful,

568
00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:15.900
something that's a little bit more authentic.

569
00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:18.890
So perhaps it's just an extension

570
00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:22.640
of that learning curve or that kind of spiritual trajectory

571
00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:25.380
that would attract somebody to the straight edge scene

572
00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:26.213
to begin with.

573
00:23:26.213 --> 00:23:28.540
But what did that look like for you?

574
00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:31.480
For me, there was not a straight edge scene

575
00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:33.020
in New York per se.

576
00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:35.183
And I was just sort of like...

577
00:23:35.183 --> 00:23:39.901
I was just more into like sports and healthy living.

578
00:23:39.901 --> 00:23:40.940
I don't know why.

579
00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:43.180
But that made you a freak among the freaks, right?

580
00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:44.760
Well, yeah, that became weird--

581
00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:46.500
You can't be into sports and be into punk.

582
00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:47.333
Exactly.

583
00:23:47.333 --> 00:23:49.780
Especially then in 1982, 'cause it was really dark

584
00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:50.980
and there was a lot of heavy drugs,

585
00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:52.600
drugs that they didn't even do in high school.

586
00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:55.650
I mean, I thought high school partying was sorta lame

587
00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:57.660
and I didn't wanna be like that.

588
00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:59.440
But when I got started hanging out in New York

589
00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:01.580
I was like, "You know, these guys are much worse."

590
00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:03.550
These guys are taking (indistinct) putting their face

591
00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:06.550
in a brown paper bag and hoffing glue.

592
00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:08.780
So what the hell is going on here?

593
00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:11.520
And I was just into like positive living,

594
00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:14.300
and I just didn't jive with that stuff.

595
00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:19.080
And so I never expected it to become as big as it was,

596
00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:21.370
but we had a band and I'm very outspoken

597
00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:22.850
about what I believe in.

598
00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:26.910
And straight edge seemed sort of quickly,

599
00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:30.962
a culture of it sort of quickly started forming.

600
00:24:30.962 --> 00:24:34.713
And then that year we got into animal rights.

601
00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:38.827
It just seemed like a natural progression of,

602
00:24:38.827 --> 00:24:41.677
"Okay, I wanna keep my mind under control,

603
00:24:41.677 --> 00:24:43.867
"my body under control, my tongue under control

604
00:24:43.867 --> 00:24:46.010
"my genitals under control."

605
00:24:46.010 --> 00:24:50.520
And I gotta figure out like, why am I killing this cow,

606
00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:52.931
but we're not killing this dog?

607
00:24:52.931 --> 00:24:56.020
When you start to think like that,

608
00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:58.187
you start to realize like, "Oh, I get it.

609
00:24:58.187 --> 00:25:01.877
"I'm like a sociopath who can sort of like,

610
00:25:01.877 --> 00:25:03.690
"'Hey, children let's play a game.'"

611
00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:05.580
Then all of a sudden you murder another child.

612
00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:08.533
So what am I doing with my mind?

613
00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:15.580
I got that in my mind living with my family.

614
00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:18.530
But I didn't know how to cook.

615
00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:19.680
So I made this deal.

616
00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.730
When I turn 18 or when I move out of my house,

617
00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:24.490
I'm not gonna eat meat anymore.

618
00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:26.200
I'm just not gonna do it.

619
00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:28.530
And then I read Peter Singer's book

620
00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:33.350
and the Krishna people also put out a book on vegetarianism,

621
00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:35.817
so then I was like, "I'm just gonna choose this lifestyle.

622
00:25:35.817 --> 00:25:37.217
"I'm just gonna deal with it.

623
00:25:37.217 --> 00:25:38.920
"And I'm gonna find support."

624
00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:40.613
Cause there's not like real support.

625
00:25:40.613 --> 00:25:42.930
You couldn't go online and find support.

626
00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.137
You just had to figure it out.

627
00:25:44.137 --> 00:25:46.140
"Okay. What am I gonna eat?"

628
00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:50.120
Spaghetti every day or Coca-Cola.

629
00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:51.600
And so it's sort of like a process.

630
00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.210
And then New York City did have that support

631
00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:55.100
with health food stores.

632
00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:56.980
I got a job at a health food restaurant.

633
00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:59.810
I started getting into yoga then.

634
00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:01.810
And that sort of--

635
00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.500
What was the yoga scene in New York like at that time?

636
00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:06.490
It all was spiritually-based yoga.

637
00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:09.147
It wasn't athletic based--

638
00:26:09.147 --> 00:26:10.116
No, of course not.

639
00:26:10.116 --> 00:26:12.533
(indistinct)

640
00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:17.874
And where it was like, I mean, David Life.

641
00:26:17.874 --> 00:26:20.031
(indistinct)

642
00:26:20.031 --> 00:26:22.170
They come from the punk movement too, right?

643
00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:23.410
Yeah, they were in the neighborhood.

644
00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:24.813
David had LifeCafe.

645
00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:27.230
He's cool.

646
00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:28.470
And Sharon's cool.

647
00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:30.370
And they live not far from me.

648
00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.500
But when you go to Jeeva Mopti now in New York

649
00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:35.869
and it's like, it's so swank and like the cafe's amazing.

650
00:26:35.869 --> 00:26:38.880
But you're like, these are just punk rockers who were like--

651
00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.660
They came out of that movement when yoga was like

652
00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:43.160
an act of rebellion.

653
00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:45.861
Yeah. And they still have that in them too.

654
00:26:45.861 --> 00:26:50.861
And yeah, they're wonderful.

655
00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:54.240
I remember practicing next to them at Dharma Mittra's place,

656
00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:58.820
Dharma Mittra's probably 85 years old this year, maybe.

657
00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:02.620
But he was long-term yoga teacher, but spiritually-based

658
00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:03.520
they all had gurus.

659
00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:08.040
And it wasn't as secular as yoga has become nowadays.

660
00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:11.000
So use it today, get some traction.

661
00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:11.877
You guys are playing all the time

662
00:27:11.877 --> 00:27:13.660
and you make a name for yourself.

663
00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:16.100
You like create this band that tons

664
00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:18.060
of people are into you start a record label.

665
00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:21.120
Like you got a lot of stuff going on by like,

666
00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.204
when is this like early 90s at this point?

667
00:27:24.204 --> 00:27:26.343
This was 1985, 86, 87.

668
00:27:28.598 --> 00:27:29.550
And then in 88,

669
00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:33.050
I started getting more serious about my spiritual life

670
00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:35.690
which sort of came from a combination

671
00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:40.690
of success and feeling like questioning what success is.

672
00:27:42.203 --> 00:27:43.243
You know, you had these bands that were sort of

673
00:27:43.243 --> 00:27:44.970
like the Beastie Boys, where they were like peers

674
00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:47.073
and were like blowing up and getting huge.

675
00:27:47.925 --> 00:27:49.310
Did you watch the documentary?

676
00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:50.463
I didn't.

677
00:27:50.463 --> 00:27:51.296
The Beastie Boys documentary?

678
00:27:51.296 --> 00:27:53.358
No I heard it was great.
It's great.

679
00:27:53.358 --> 00:27:55.580
I mean, you would love it because they have so much footage

680
00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:57.010
from the very early days, right?

681
00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:59.470
Yeah. I remember being on tour and they were on tour

682
00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:03.430
and I saw them when they left New York at Webster Hall.

683
00:28:03.430 --> 00:28:06.620
And it was just like, I remember when the record licensed

684
00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:08.351
to ill started blowing up

685
00:28:08.351 --> 00:28:09.430
and I was like, this is unbelievable.

686
00:28:09.430 --> 00:28:11.700
This is actually unbelievable.

687
00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:13.217
And then we were on tour

688
00:28:13.217 --> 00:28:15.380
and we saw them in LA and, you know

689
00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:18.510
run DMC or there, and I'm like, this is unbelievable.

690
00:28:18.510 --> 00:28:19.343
And it was just like

691
00:28:19.343 --> 00:28:20.230
the whole thing was just shocking.

692
00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:22.730
And our front that Murphy's Law was a friends of ours

693
00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:24.840
and they were on tour with them too.

694
00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.120
And the whole thing it was like shocking, like,

695
00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:29.710
cause no one's expecting--

696
00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:30.760
We're just having fun.

697
00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:33.480
Like we're doing DIY stuff, we're printing our own

698
00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:35.936
you know, flyers and all that kind of stuff.

699
00:28:35.936 --> 00:28:36.910
Like there was no expectation

700
00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:40.160
or attachment to any kind of like broader success.

701
00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:41.750
Or this is a career.

702
00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:42.583
This is a career.

703
00:28:42.583 --> 00:28:44.790
No one thought of I, when I grow up, I wanna be in a band.

704
00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:45.950
It wasn't like that at all.

705
00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:50.155
And never, we just sort of like, I don't know, on a mission.

706
00:28:50.155 --> 00:28:51.360
I don't know what it was.

707
00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:55.720
So yeah, that was a little shocking when that happened,

708
00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:57.610
but what was your question I forgot?

709
00:28:57.610 --> 00:28:58.870
Basically like you,

710
00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:01.160
I wanna walk you up to this point where you end

711
00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:03.460
up walking away from the band and everything.

712
00:29:03.460 --> 00:29:06.633
So the band's getting bigger, you're getting more success.

713
00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:09.060
You know, that you're starting to...

714
00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:11.050
My father got sick and he died.

715
00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:14.320
Now it seems like that was the big inflection point.

716
00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:17.423
And I think sometimes it takes a tragedy to sort of,

717
00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:22.190
to make you reevaluate life.

718
00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:23.900
These spiritual epiphany is like--

719
00:29:23.900 --> 00:29:26.438
But you'd been doing yoga?

720
00:29:26.438 --> 00:29:29.590
I had been into it and sort of on a spiritual quest

721
00:29:29.590 --> 00:29:34.590
in this had really exacerbated or not exacerbated

722
00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:39.980
but just Slingshot at my desire to go deeper

723
00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:42.760
cause I really felt like there is nothing

724
00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:46.700
of this world that will really satisfy my heart.

725
00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:48.150
There is no amount of success.

726
00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:49.593
There's no soulmate.

727
00:29:51.725 --> 00:29:55.450
There's this beautiful statement

728
00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:59.510
in Bhakti literature that there's, you know

729
00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:01.775
this is how I phrase it anyway there,

730
00:30:01.775 --> 00:30:03.770
you know, there's a, God-shaped hole, a God-shaped hole

731
00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.727
in the heart and we'll shove anything in that hole.

732
00:30:06.727 --> 00:30:09.160
And we're looking to shove anything to feel connected

733
00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:11.620
but only God will fit in that hole.

734
00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:13.650
And we try to the three main things

735
00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:17.160
that we would try to put in that hole are we feel

736
00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.740
like there is a person out there that will make me complete

737
00:30:22.022 --> 00:30:23.940
and it's been romanticized.

738
00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:25.820
And there are people that we connect with.

739
00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:29.330
There people that we love, but they're not our God.

740
00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:30.460
They can't be our God.

741
00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:31.930
And we shouldn't make them our God

742
00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:34.730
or we're setting ourselves up for hell in the future.

743
00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:36.670
And or we're gonna become incredibly needy

744
00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:37.590
or they're gonna be needy.

745
00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:40.760
It's gonna be a real dysfunctional relationship.

746
00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:42.450
So the person shouldn't be our God.

747
00:30:42.450 --> 00:30:43.603
And the next one is,

748
00:30:47.141 --> 00:30:51.141
there's no amount of financial height you can go

749
00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:56.122
to that will actually satisfy that yourself too.

750
00:30:56.122 --> 00:30:58.743
There's no amount of money can put them at God-shaped hole.

751
00:30:58.743 --> 00:31:01.430
And the final one is there's no an amount

752
00:31:01.430 --> 00:31:04.360
of validation, material validation.

753
00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:07.800
You can find people who sometimes get the most validation

754
00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:10.950
and they're sometimes even more screwed up.

755
00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:12.630
And I started feeling like, yeah

756
00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:14.300
there's nothing of this world.

757
00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:17.930
And I remember sitting in a yoga center once

758
00:31:17.930 --> 00:31:22.060
and reading this quote from a Swami saying

759
00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:24.143
you must become materially exhausted,

760
00:31:25.430 --> 00:31:27.910
to start your spiritual life.

761
00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:32.910
You have to like be cynical with material success.

762
00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:36.360
Give up hope.

763
00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:38.070
And that was always a very positive person.

764
00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:39.770
Matter of fact, I'd read these books early

765
00:31:39.770 --> 00:31:41.610
on my life and think, oh, these yogis

766
00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:44.060
they're so negative about life.

767
00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:44.893
What's wrong with life.

768
00:31:44.893 --> 00:31:45.726
Life is good.

769
00:31:45.726 --> 00:31:46.923
I've got so much hope,

770
00:31:46.923 --> 00:31:51.300
but you have to be cynical with material hope.

771
00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:52.400
You can be very, very

772
00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:56.380
you can believe in spiritual fortune.

773
00:31:56.380 --> 00:31:58.930
Like you're, there is a spiritual success

